**Search and rejection summary**
- I considered roughly 30 documents across the constitution text, Reuters/Euronews, CPJ, OHCHR, NetBlocks, Access Now, Amnesty, HRW, and UN materials.
- I kept sources that added distinct mechanisms: formal election supervision, nominal rights with carve-outs, journalist arrests, protest incitement, internet shutdowns, minority-region repression, and later accountability findings.
- I rejected duplicate syndications of the same Reuters wire when they added no new factual claim.
- I rejected broader 2023-2026 retrospectives when they were not directly tied to the 2022 period, except where they summarized 2022 shutdowns or accountability gaps.
- I did not re-fetch Freedom House, RSF, V-Dem, WGI, BTI, or Polity because the local prior already supplied the structured baseline and the instructions prohibited re-fetching those datasets.
- I did not chase out-of-period election stories because 2022 had no national contest that changed the core picture.
- I avoided padding with unrelated macro or nuclear material because it does not answer Chapter 4B.

**Remaining gaps**
- Direct evidence of Khamenei personally ordering every arrest, internet cutoff, or court action is still limited; attribution is strongest at the system level.
- There is no 2022 election event to test contestability in practice, so 4B.1 and 4B.2 rest mainly on constitutional structure plus the repression record.
- Better Persian-language official records on Guardian Council actions, judiciary directives, or media closures would sharpen attribution.
- The main substantive gap is not severity but granularity: the record already supports a closed, coercive political system, but the personal command chain can still be tightened.
